LIBRARY OF CONSISTENT TERMS
Use each term exactly as written. Do not swap in synonyms.
Format: TERM | what it means / what it is used for | where first coined

--- THE THESIS ---
the present thesis | this thesis; used when emphasizing alone as well as alongside other things non included in this thesis to draw a clear line.
the author | refers to the author and reasearcher and experiment lead of the present thesis
beyond the scope of the present thesis | fixed phrase for a question the present thesis names but does not test; pair with a pointer to Future Research | Sec. Participants

--- THE CONSTRUCT ---
engagement | the primary construct label of the thesis; everything else is an indicator of it | Sec. Defining Engagement (Intro)
working definition | the screen-and-swipe definition of engagement, boxed in the Introduction | Sec. Defining Engagement (Intro)
updated definition | the instrument-free version of the definition | Sec. An Updated Definition of Engagement
disengagement event / exit | the observable moment engagement ends (in this thesis: the swipe) | Sec. Defining Engagement (Intro)
exit criterion | what would have counted as disengagement; one of the four reporting requirements | Sec. Implications for Measurement
declared interval | the timescale over which engagement is claimed | Sec. The Proposed Definition
occupancy | attention with no alternative (captive), or presence without engagement; NOT engagement | Sec. The Proposed Definition
normative dissociation | zoned-out, low-recall scrolling (Baughan et al.) | Sec. Engagement and Attention in short-form video platforms

--- THE SETTING ---
infinite feed | the short-form video feed itself, where participants are | Sec. Adjacent studies
naturalistic use | self-paced viewing of an algorithmic feed, with a swipe possible at any moment (in this thesis: TikTok For You feed on the author's iPhone, reset before each participant; NOT 'own feed on own phone') | Sec. Adjacent studies
swipe | the single self-paced action that moves to the next video; anchors every label | Intro / Sec. Adjacent studies
burst / burst block | several rapid swipes merged into one block; swipes merge when their ±3.0 s burst windows overlap (closer than 6.0 s); the burst window is NOT the SKIP period | Sec. Behavioral Burst Analysis
burst window | ±3.0 s around each swipe, used only by the burst analysis; groups swipes, changes no label; separate definition from the SKIP period, though the two can overlap in time | Sec. Behavioral Burst Analysis
inter-swipe interval | time between two consecutive "A" keypresses, roughly the viewing time of one video (do NOT use "dwell time" for it) | Sec. Behavioral Burst Analysis
block size | number of swipes in one burst block; a burst block of size 1 is a swipe with no other swipe within 6.0 s; do NOT write "n" for it (n is the sample size) and do NOT use "burst group"; always say whether a share counts SWIPES (40.3% stand alone at ±3.0 s) or BURST BLOCKS (67.4% have size 1 at ±3.0 s) | Sec. Behavioral Swipe-Burst Phenomenon
fast scrollers / slow scrollers | participants with many large burst blocks vs mostly burst blocks of size 1; the two ends of one range, not two groups (do NOT use "frantic scrollers" or "careful scrollers") | Sec. Behavioral Swipe-Burst Phenomenon

--- THE LITERATURE ---
adjacent study | Rahman et al. 2025; same Muse S headband, different question | Sec. Adjacent studies
secondary adjacent studies | the three compared EEG studies of short-form video viewing without a consumer headband: Zhang et al., Du et al., Zhang and Zhang | Sec. Secondary adjacent studies
black box vs glass box | detection-accuracy model (adjacent study) vs model that shows which part of the signal carries the class information (this thesis) | Sec. Adjacent studies
punching a hole | mental image for a notch filter removing 50 Hz and everything real near it | Sec. Adjacent studies
powerline-noise | 50 Hz electrical contamination (use this, NOT "mains frequency", "line noise", "power-line noise") | Sec. Adjacent studies
door closing | mental image for the swipe as the datable end of engagement (vs a score as a dimmer switch) | Sec. Defining Engagement: Existing Definitions and Their Gaps
DBCI | digital behaviour change intervention, e.g. a health app or health website | Sec. Engagement in the Health Communication Literature
health-engagement literature | DBCI research plus research on health messages on TikTok, taken together | Sec. Engagement in the Health Communication Literature
ask route / log route / body route | the three routes to measure engagement (Perski et al.): self-report / automated usage recording / physiological or psychophysical correlates; EEG in this thesis is a body-route measure | Sec. Engagement in the Health Communication Literature
dose | the counts from the log route (use, exposure, platform counters) | Sec. Engagement in the Health Communication Literature

--- THE RECORDING ---
baseline phase / browsing phase | the two phases of a session: 2-minute resting video / 25-minute TikTok browsing | Sec. Experimental Protocol
scrolling task | the free TikTok browsing of the browsing phase, as explained to participants | Sec. Lessons from Running the Experiment
briefing | the instructions the author gave participants during the session (keypress at each swipe, start and end of the scrolling task, keep scrolling after a Bluetooth dropout; pause or end only if unwell) | Sec. Lessons from Running the Experiment
bridge | muselsl; carries the Muse Bluetooth signal to the laptop and onto LSL; mental image: a bridge between headband and laptop | Sec. Recording Software
shared clock | LSL time; one clock on the wall that every stream (EEG, keypresses) reads | Sec. Recording Software
recording pipeline | the thesis's own recording code (one Python file, recording_script.py): finds the headband, starts muselsl, reads EEG with pylsl, flags keypresses in LSL time, reconnects after a Bluetooth dropout (do NOT use "custom recording script", "recording architecture" or "Universal"; section title: Reproducible Recording Pipeline) | Sec. Recording Software / Reproducible Recording Pipeline
recording file | one CSV file of the recording pipeline; a Bluetooth dropout ends one recording file and starts the next; the preprocessing pipeline processes each recording file on its own and never joins two (do NOT use "CSV file" in running text) | Sec. Reproducible Preprocessing Pipeline
preprocessing pipeline | the thesis's own preprocessing code (step01-step03): trims, interpolates to 256 Hz, splits into bands, z-scores band envelopes against the baseline, cuts windows; runs once without and once with a 50 Hz notch filter (section title: Reproducible Preprocessing Pipeline; do NOT write "Pre-Processing") | Sec. Reproducible Preprocessing Pipeline
full pipeline | all code of the thesis, from recording to results: reproducible recording pipeline (3.5), reproducible preprocessing pipeline (3.6), feature engineering (3.7), result synthesis pipeline (3.8); public at github.com/coffeeandcoffee/eeg_tiktok_pipeline (do NOT use "analysis and recording pipeline" or "methodological pipeline") | Sec. The Reusable Pipeline
reconnect gap | lost time between two recording files after a Bluetooth dropout; logged, never crossed by a window, never interpolated | Sec. Recording Software

--- THE LABELS ---
STAY | the label of windows drawn from the STAY period: continuous, non-skip viewing inside the infinite feed | Sec. Operational Definition of STAY and SKIP
SKIP | the label of windows drawn from the SKIP period before a swipe | Sec. Operational Definition of STAY and SKIP
labeling parameters | the four values that define the STAY and SKIP labels: pre-swipe gap, SKIP period, post-swipe gap, window length; the sensitivity analysis varies the first, second and fourth, the post-swipe gap stays at 0.5 s (do NOT use "timing values" or "temporal parameters") | Sec. Labeling Strategy
SKIP period | 3.0 s just before the pre-swipe gap, from which SKIP windows are drawn (do NOT use "imminent-skip period") | Sec. Operational Definition of Labeling Parameters
pre-swipe gap | 2.0 s excluded right before the keypress that marks a swipe; meant to keep swipe preparation out of SKIP (do NOT use "gap length") | Sec. Operational Definition of Labeling Parameters
window length | 1.0 s analysis window | Sec. Operational Definition of Labeling Parameters
post-swipe gap | 0.5 s excluded right after the keypress that marks a swipe (do NOT use "grace period") | Sec. Operational Definition of Labeling Parameters
STAY period | all remaining browsing time: starts after a post-swipe gap, ends where the next SKIP period starts | Sec. Operational Definition of STAY and SKIP
Bereitschaftspotential (BP) | slow brain signal of movement preparation before a voluntary action | Sec. Pre-Motor Window

--- THE SIGNAL AND FEATURES ---
envelope | the strength of one band at every moment (Hilbert transform); mental image: a line traced along the tops of the waves; not squared to power; the band envelopes are z-scored, the raw signal is NOT | Sec. Baseline Extraction and Normalization
artifact check | the 95th-percentile peak-to-peak flagging of blinks (AF7/AF8, 1-40 Hz) and EMG (TP9/TP10, 30-100 Hz) on the notch-filtered copy; flags only, removes nothing | Sec. Artifact Analysis
slice | one view of the data: one band, one electrode, one statistic, or a combination; a slice contains multiple features | Sec. Adjacent studies
feature | a part of a slice, defined by specific band, electrode, statistic, and or combination.
raw signal | the undecomposed broadband signal, kept alongside the seven bands | Sec. Baseline Extraction and Normalization
high-gamma (40-60 Hz) / very high gamma (60-100 Hz) | the two top bands, both reaching into powerline-noise territory | Sec. Baseline Extraction and Normalization
Peak Frequency / Macro Frequency | the two self-defined time-domain frequency features | Sec. Peak and Macro Frequency Extraction
Engagement Index (EI) | the classical ratio beta/(alpha+theta); the benchmark | Intro / Sec. Engagement Index Calculation
Cohen's d | class separation of one feature, in units of that feature's own spread | Sec. Adjacent studies / Class Separation Metric
top features | TP10 raw standard deviation, AF7 high-gamma mean, AF8 delta peak frequency (write the names like this in running text, NOT the table names 'TP10 raw std' etc.) | Sec. Feature Separation Analysis / Class Separation of Single Features
Alternative Index | RETIRED (2026-09-28): do NOT use; say 'top features' / 'top-feature models' | Sec. Top-Feature Models
coin-flip baseline | chance-level reference classifier | Sec. Logistic Regression Modeling and Baselines
intra-subject / inter-subject | within one participant (blocked 5-fold) / across participants (Leave-One-Group-Out); do not use only Inter or Intra, not Inter-Subject and Intra-Subject, instead always say Inter-subject and Intra-subject! | Sec. Temporal Cross-Validation

